      "observed": "All eight walk and eight run windows show changing, alternating leg and foot poses across consecutive capture rows. The upper body keeps its weapon aimed while travel directions change. Run windows show wider/faster leg-phase changes and faster floor-grid passage than the walk samples.",
      "qualification": "The gait still looks mechanical: knee and foot changes are sharp, legs cross or nearly overlap in side-facing views, and the upper aim pose remains rigid. No persistent gross whole-leg floor penetration is visible in these selected views. This does not establish exact world-space planted-foot stability, all start/stop transitions, or polished natural human weight."
    },
    {
      "id": "turns",
      "windows": [
        "turn_cw",
        "turn_ccw"
      ],
      "observed": "The gun and torso move through intermediate orientations in both turn directions. The feet stay close and fairly quiet through part of each turn, then make a brief following step/crossing change as the body turns.",
      "qualification": "The stationary base through part of the turn and the quick following foot changes remain visible. These sheets do not establish smooth continuous weight transfer or exact planting."
    },
    {
      "id": "pivots",
      "windows": [
        "pivot_first",
        "pivot_second",
        "pivot_third"
      ],
      "observed": "Each pivot shows a sharp upper-body and weapon reversal between adjacent captured rows, followed by a short foot/cross-step change and settling.",
      "qualification": "Aim changes are visibly prompt, but the transition reads as an abrupt pose change. It should not be described as a silky or fully weighted turn, or as a measurement of native mouse responsiveness."
    },
    {
      "id": "attack_families",
      "windows": [
        "swipe",
        "rake",
        "twohand"
      ],
      "observed": "All three explicit family windows show raised-arm preparation, knee bend/forward step, torso incline, reach/contact and recovery. Red directional damage feedback and blood appear near the contact beat. The two-handed family has paired forearms raised together and a clearer different silhouette; swipe and rake remain similar at this overhead angle, with the rake arc held higher near the head for longer.",
      "qualification": "These explicit standstill family fixtures do not establish ordinary crowded approach or the complete run-to-strike transition. A short player upper-body reaction is visible, but static sheets cannot establish whether controls feel interrupted. Exact contact counts and health changes are automated evidence, separate from this visual judgment."
    },
    {
